nto the multi-benchmark analysis, I need to track how the variance components shift across different task types—math reasoning shows much lower single-model item effects around 28% but temperature interactions become prominent at 15%, suggesting reasoning tasks are more sensitive to sampling variation than knowledge-based tasks. Now I'm looking at the variance breakdown for HellaSwag, which shows item effects dominating at 50% with cross-model variation around 35%. Across all four benchmarks, there's a clear gradient from MMLU down to GSM8K with a 33 percentage point spread that aligns with the knowledge-to-reasoning spectrum. The hardware experiments confirm that infrastructure has minimal impact on results, with GPU type accounting for less than 0.3% of variance, validating that the model and item effects are the primary drivers.\n\nFor the comprehensive analysis, I'm running bootstrap resampling across all variance components and comparing different estimation approaches—IRT shows substantially higher variance than G-theory, but REML and Henderson's method agree closely, and the results hold up across fixed versus random specifications. The binary scoring analysis shows moderate correlation with the observed data, confirming it's not an artifact of the measurement approach. At scale with 14K MMLU items, generalizability reaches 0.94, and the power analysis indicates I can reliably detect interaction effects above 5%.\n\nMoving into the 14B model experiments, the variance structure mirrors what I saw with the 8B models, though item effects are slightly elevated due to more consistent model behavior.\n\nThe fine-tuning regime tells a different story—seed and learning rate become the dominant sources of variance at around 35% combined, while item effects drop to 25-30%, suggesting the training process introduces more systematic variation than the inference-time factors.\n\nLayerCast substantially reduces precision-related variance by roughly 60%, though other components remain largely unchanged. When I shift to the MATH dataset, item effects fall even lower than GSM8K at around 22%, and chain-of-thought reasoning amplifies the temperature-by-item interaction.\n\nWriting YAML structure... slightly lower to account for averaging effects across more models). So my predictions for the 4-model scenario are: model×item around 24.1%, cross-model G around 0.065, and single-model item_id around 59% on average.\n\nNow I need to clarify the precision setup—Gemma only supports bf16 and fp32, so the cross-model analysis will use that balanced subset across all four models, while fp16 data from the other models gets used in their individual within-model analyses.\n\nLet me work through the record counts: for each model with bf16+fp32, that's 2 precisions times 6 prompts, 6 seeds, 3 temperatures, and 4 orderings, giving 864 conditions per model times 200 items equals 172,800 records, so 691,200 total across the four models for the cross-model G-study. The single-model analyses for Llama, Mistral, and Qwen include all three precisions, which bumps up to 1,296 conditions per model and 259,200 records each.\n\nNow looking at the actual data, exp001_llama shows 239,200 records with 1,196 conditions—that's close to but not exactly 1,296, so some condition combinations must have been excluded or not run. Let me figure out what's actually in the dataset rather than assuming the full factorial design was executed.\n\nFor the G-theory paper, I need to plan the core tables: variance decomposition showing all sources and their percent contributions with confidence intervals, a G-coefficient summary comparing across conditions, and D-study projections. For exp-001 specifically, I'll want separate tables for single-model variance decomposition across the four models with binary scoring, then cross-model results, followed by G-coefficient comparisons.\n\nExp-002 will need variance decomposition across benchmarks with corresponding G-coefficients and D-study projections per benchmark. Exp-003 requires hardware-crossed variance decomposition and separability tests for interaction terms. Exp-004 is analysis-only with IRT comparisons, bootstrap confidence intervals, and sensitivity analyses.\n\nNow I'm writing out the YAML structure to capture all this. I need to verify the P0 experiments are complete on time—that's five experiments total (exp-001, exp-001b, exp-002, exp-003, exp-004)—and exp-004 depends on exp-001 since it's analysis-only with no GPU requirements.\n\nFor the best-case scenario, I'll outline how each P0 experiment contributes to the narrative: the 4-model data validates the 2-model trends, resampling confirms stability, cross-benchmark gradients become clear, multi-hardware separability is confirmed, and the comprehensive analysis includes all defense mechanisms. I should also provide predictions for P1 and P2 experiments even though the deadline only strictly requires P0 completion.\n\nWriting YAML output... I'm realizing I need to be more careful about how these variance components add up—they should total close to 100%. Let me reorganize the single-model results properly, starting with the item effects and then accounting for the other sources of variation.\n\nFor the cross-model case with four models and binary scoring on MMLU, the picture gets more complex with model effects, model-by-item interactions, and various precision and prompt interactions all contributing to the overall variance, along with some smaller infrastructure and method effects.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how to structure the actual predictions—the user wants detailed data for each planned experiment with clear rationales for every prediction, so I need to be thorough about laying out the key metrics and the reasoning behind them rather than trying to perfectly balance a variance decomposition table.\n\nLet me count through the experiments and organize the YAML output carefully, making sure the predictions are internally consistent. For the benchmark gradient predictions, I'm considering how different tasks reveal different patterns—MMLU tests factual knowledge retrieval where item difficulty is relatively stable across models since it depends on training data coverage, while HellaSwag tests commonsense reasoning where there's more model-specificity in learned patterns.\n\nARC-Challenge requires multi-step scientific reasoning that varies more with methodological factors, and GSM8K is most sensitive to prompt formatting and sampling variance. This creates a clean gradient where MMLU shows the highest item_id effect, followed by HellaSwag, then ARC, with GSM8K lowest. The 33 percentage point spread between MMLU and GSM8K fits within the expected range, and the perfect rank correlation checks out.\n\nAs item_id decreases across benchmarks, the interaction effects with temperature, prompt, and seed should increase—especially for GSM8K where chain-of-thought randomness and format sensitivity matter most. Model×item effects should remain substantial across all benchmarks since different models have varying factual knowledge and mathematical capabilities, so I'm expecting model×item to stay above 20% even in the best tier.\n\nThe cross-model G values are consistently low across benchmarks, which reinforces the narrative about G collapse. Now I'm ready to construct the YAML with the actual numbers, though I need to reconcile the condition counts for the Llama experiments since the theoretical maximum doesn't quite match the existing data. I'm getting caught up in the exact record counts when what really matters is understanding the experimental design and key findings. Let me shift focus to the metrics and reasoning behind each experiment, then structure the output clearly. For exp-004 especially, I need to highlight the major sub-analyses that would be most relevant to the paper's narrative.\n\nWriting the output now... For the resampling experiment, I'm working through the variance metrics across three samples with 32,400 total records. The item_id accuracy varies slightly across samples, and I need to verify the coefficient of variation is within the expected range—it's coming out to about 0.020, which sits at the lower bound, so I'll adjust it to 0.038 to be more centered in the [0.02, 0.08] range. I need to settle on concrete sample values that yield a CV around 0.04, which represents reasonably stable item_id variance across the three resampled datasets. Let me work with samples like [57.5%, 62.3%, 60.2%] that give me the right coefficient of variation, then I can think through what this means for the exp-001b design with its three independent stratified samples of 200 MMLU items. those percentages look more realistic with a bit more spread. Now I'm moving on to the multi-benchmark experiment and realizing there's a precision mismatch—Gemma doesn't support fp16, so I'd need to adjust the precision levels depending on which model I'm testing. The GPU budget of 200 hours aligns with the scale of this experiment. Looking at the variance decomposition across benchmarks, GSM8K shows strong single-model item effects at 28.3% with moderate cross-model variation at 16.5%, while ARC-Challenge has higher item variance at 42.1% and cross-model effects at 27.4%, suggesting different difficulty distributions between tasks. Now I'm looking at how the single-model item effects rank across benchmarks, with MMLU showing the strongest gradient at 60.4% down to GSM8K at 28.3%—that 32-percentage-point spread fits the target range and the perfect rank correlation confirms the knowledge-to-reasoning ordering. For the multi-hardware experiment, I'm setting up a larger factorial design across GPU types and precisions to measure how hardware interactions affect model performance, with predictions showing relatively small main effects but notable item-level interactions.\n\nMoving into the comprehensive analysis, I'm checking whether item response theory or generalizability theory better captures the variance structure—IRT needs roughly a quarter of the items that G-theory requires, and the bootstrap confidence intervals on the model-by-item effects exclude zero, confirming the interaction is real. I'm also comparing different estimation approaches like REML versus Henderson's method and testing how sensitive the results are to treating item effects as fixed versus random, plus examining how well the Bernoulli variance structure aligns with observed item difficulty patterns.\n\nContinuing through the remaining experiments, the bootstrap and jackknife confidence intervals track closely together, the MMLU results hold up whether I use a 2×2 or 3×3 design, and the generalizability coefficient reaches 0.943 across the full 14K items with power exceeding 99.5% for detecting interaction effects at this scale. Scaling up to 14B models preserves the variance hierarchy I found in the 8B case with item effects still around 62%, though the larger models produce slightly more stable estimates. But fine-tuning reveals a completely different picture—the learning rate and seed interactions become the dominant variance source at 28%, item effects drop to 32%, and data shuffling introduces new sources of variation that don't appear during inference.\n\nWhen I apply LayerCast precision improvements, the precision effect shrinks by about 65% and the precision-by-item interaction drops from 2.8% to 1.0%, while the generalizability coefficient improves slightly. Across the MATH benchmark, item effects are the lowest I've seen at 22%, but chain-of-thought reasoning creates a substantial temperature-by-item interaction at 14.2%, and this extends the gradient I've been tracking—MMLU shows the highest item variance, then HellaSwag, ARC, GSM8K, and finally MATH with the lowest, confirming that the benchmark itself shapes the variance structure.\n\nNow I'm ready to compile this into the full YAML configuration, though I should double-check the design specifications for experiment 002 first.\n\nFor the precision handling across models, I need to decide how to handle Gemma since it doesn't support FP16—I'll assume it uses BF16 and FP32 to maintain a balanced two-level design across all models. Let me work through the total record counts more carefully to ensure the design is sound.\n\nLooking at the actual numbers from what's been completed and what's planned, I'm noticing a discrepancy between the theoretical condition count (1,296) and what Llama actually produced (1,196 conditions from 239,200 records). That 100-condition gap might come from the pilot phase using only float32 and T=0.0, which could have been excluded or consolidated somehow. I need to figure out whether this gap is systematic across all models or if it's specific to how the pilot was structured.\n\nFor the total dataset across all models, I'm looking at roughly 890,000 records when I add up Llama's 239,200, Gemma's 172,800, Mistral's expected ~239,200, and Qwen3's planned ~239,200. The GPU budget constraint is real—Llama already consumed significant time, and I need to verify there's enough capacity for all four models at the best tier. Since Mistral is already running, I'll assume the budget works out.\n\nNow I need to stop second-guessing and actually write the YAML configuration. I'm also thinking through the D-study projections for MMLU, where I expect to achieve G≥0.80 at around 480 items for the practical scenario with precision and temperature fixed.\n\nFor the all-random condition, I'd need roughly 540 items to hit the same threshold. The other benchmarks show different patterns—HellaSwag needs about 380 items in the practical case versus 450 for all-random. But I'm realizing that when item_id is lower, items contribute less signal, which actually means I need MORE items to compensate for that noise, not fewer. When I fix additional facets like precision and temperature, I'm removing noise sources, but benchmarks with weaker item effects still require larger D-study budgets to achieve the same generalizability coefficient.\n\nSo the sample sizes scale with the noise profile: MMLU at 480 items, HellaSwag at 550, ARC at 650, and GSM8K at 900—basically, benchmarks requiring more reasoning have noisier evaluations and need bigger budgets. For cross-model studies, even 10 models only gives me a G of 0.179, and dropping to 4 models tanks it to 0.065, which shows that model variance is largely irreducible. When I compare IRT and G-theory approaches on MMLU, IRT needs just 116 items for reliable measurement while G-theory requires 510—a 4.4× difference that really highlights how much more conservative the generalizability framework is.\n\nThe gap comes down to what each method treats as noise: G-theory captures prompt×item interactions (4.6%), seed×item effects (4.7%), ordering effects, and residual variance that IRT lumps into measurement error. Looking across all benchmarks, the ratios hold pretty consistently—MMLU at 4.4×, HellaSwag at 4.4×, ARC at 4.5×, GSM8K at 4.5×—which suggests this 4.0–4.5× range is robust. GSM8K's lower IRT requirement makes sense because math items tend to have higher discrimination, so fewer are needed to pin down ability, but G-theory still demands many more items to account for all the prompt and seed variability that comes with reasoning tasks.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the YAML configuration with these ratios locked in, and I need to decide whether to structure the expected results as nested dictionaries or flat key-value pairs based on the format shown.\n\nFor the tables and figures, I'll describe their structure and content as strings rather than trying to reproduce them literally. The analysis field for each experiment should capture both the methodological approach and the main findings.\n\nLet me work through the record count for exp-002 more carefully—I'm multiplying out the design factors: 4 models, 2 precision settings (with Gemma using BF16/FP32 instead), 6 prompt variations, 6 random seeds, 2 temperature settings, 4 orderings, 200 items per benchmark, and 3 benchmarks total.\n\nNow I'm calculating whether this is actually feasible within the 200 GPU hour budget. Each inference condition across 200 items likely takes 5-20 minutes depending on the model and benchmark, which would put the total somewhere around 1,152 GPU hours at a conservative 10 minutes per condition—that's significantly over budget. I need to reconsider whether the design constraints are tighter than my initial calculation suggests.\n\nActually, with vLLM batching, processing 200 items in a single batch could be much faster, maybe just 1-2 minutes per condition. That would bring the total down to around 230 GPU hours, which fits within the 200-hour budget with some room for overhead. For GSM8K specifically, chain-of-thought reasoning might push it to 5 minutes per condition, but the exact timing isn't critical for making predictions about the experiment design.\n\nNow I'm noticing the exp-002 description specifies BF16/FP16 precision, but Gemma doesn't support FP16. I'll assume Gemma uses BF16/FP32 instead to maintain the two-precision setup across all models. That gives me 4 models × 2 precisions × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 2 temperatures...\n\nWriting YAML config... Writing YAML configuration... 现在规划一个大规模的4模型MMLU精度研究，涵盖多个精度设置、提示词、随机种子和温度参数，目标是用4个模型验证之前2模型发现的模型与题目交互效应，预期生成约89万条记录，各模型在bfloat16精度下的准确率分别在68.5%到74.8%之间。\n\n方差分析显示题目ID是最主要的变异来源（36.8%），其次是模型与题目的交互（24.1%），而模型本身的差异相对较小（8.3%），其他因素如精度、提示词和种子的影响都在3%以下。\n\n四模型的泛化系数为0.065，十模型的泛化系数提升到0.179，实现80%可靠性需要480个精心选择的题目或540个随机题目。核心验证逻辑是通过引入Mistral和Qwen3来扩大模型架构多样性——包括decoder-only、独特的归一化结构和MoE注意力变体——来检验两模型趋势的稳健性。\n\n单模型的item方差维持在55-65%，反映了MMLU子集中STEM、人文和社科题目的内在难度异质性。跨模型的泛化系数略降至0.065是因为更多模型引入了额外的model×item交互源，而D-study表明即使十个模型也只能达到0.179的泛化系数，证明model×item交互是系统性的——源于模型架构和训练数据差异导致的item难度重排，而非采样噪声。\n\n在V0强制eager执行消除引擎混淆后，基础设施与方法的交互降至0.1%以下，因为二值评分下的精度差异主要影响绝对水平而非交互结构。现在我在整理方差分解表格，展示单模型和跨模型的方差来源分布。\n\n单模型分析中项目ID占比约60%，跨模型分析显示项目×模型交互占24.1%、项目ID占36.8%、模型主效应仅8.3%。G系数对比表明单模型平均值为0.618，而4模型跨模型分析降至0.065，要达到0.80的可靠性需要480个项目。\n\n我正在分析核心流程：对4个模型分别运行单因素G研究确认项目ID方差范围，然后合并数据以模型作为交叉随机因子重新分析，通过D研究投影在固定精度和温度参数后，模拟从100到2000个项目时的G系数曲线，并与2模型结果对比。\n\n由于Gemma仅有bf16和fp32数据，跨模型分析采用这两种精度的平衡子集。接下来我要验证项目支配性发现的稳健性，通过3组独立分层抽样的200个MMLU项目（使用不同随机种子）与Llama模型交叉，测试多个精度、提示和排序条件组合，确保这个发现不是特定样本的伪影。\n\n现在我在查看这三组样本的结果指标，项目ID百分比在57.8到62.5之间波动，均值为60.13，变异系数仅0.039，远低于15%的阈值，说明分层抽样有效保证了每组200个项目覆盖相同的学科分布。\n\nG系数的稳定性（CV=0.030）源于它本质上是项目ID的函数，项目ID稳定则G稳定。样本间的微小波动反映了具体项目的随机变异——某些样本可能抽到更多高区分度的项目如高等数学，略微推高项目ID，但整体结构在所有样本中保持一致。\n\n现在我在检验这种稳定性，通过对三组独立项目样本分别运行完整的G-study，计算每个方差成分的变异系数，并检验各来源的排序是否在样本间保持不变。\n\n如果变异系数小于0.08且排序保持一致，就能确认项目主导性是MMLU的结构特征而非抽样伪影。接下来我要设计一个跨基准的复制实验，在GSM8K、ARC-Challenge和HellaSwag上用多个模型、精度、提示词和种子进行大规模测试，预计生成超过138万条记录来检验方差梯度的一致性。\n\n现在我在查看各基准上的具体方差分解结果，HellaSwag显示单个模型内的项目差异占50.4%，这个比例在不同基准间存在显著差异。 Now I'm looking at the generalizability metrics for GSM8K and seeing how the variance components break down differently across benchmarks. The data shows that single-model item effects are weaker here compared to ARC, while cross-model interactions are also lower, suggesting different sources of variability. I'm also noting that the benchmarks show separability below the 0.5% threshold, and I'm examining the rationale for why gradient patterns decrease monotonically across benchmarks—starting with MMLU's focus on closed-form factual knowledge where item difficulty depends on knowledge rarity. 这对同一模型的不同prompt/seed/temperature相对稳定——模型要么在训练数据中见过这个知识点，要么没见过。因此item_id占主导。\n\nHellaSwag(常识推理, 50.4%): 题目需要基于日常场景的推理。虽然item难度仍有较强的固有结构(某些场景本质上更容易判断)，但prompt措辞影响模型对场景的\"理解角度\"，引入了额外的prompt×item方差(5.4% vs MMLU 3.2%)，压低了item_id的相对占比。\n\nARC-Challenge(科学推理, 42.1%)需要多步推理链，其中prompt引导和随机采样的影响显著增大，prompt×item和seed×item的交互方差分别达到7.3%和5.8%。不同模型的科学推理能力差异更大(model=9.8%)，进一步稀释了item_id的占比。\n\nGSM8K(数学推理, 28.3%)中，多步算术和chain-of-thought推理使得temperature对推理链的随机性影响极大(temperature×item=9.4%)，而prompt格式(特别是few-shot示例选择)对解题路径的影响也很关键(prompt×item=11.8%)，导致item_id最低，因为同一题目在不同prompt/seed/temperature组合下的表现差异最大。\n\n现在看model×item在所有benchmark上维持20-24%的原因：不同模型的训练数据和架构差异导致它们在不同类型的item上有系统性的强弱，这种model-specific的item ranking在所有benchmark上都产生显著的交互，不受task type调节。D-study预算的梯度(MMLU 480 → GSM8K 900 items)反映了推理型benchmark的非item方差来源更多更大。\n\n这些额外噪声需要更多items来稀释，才能达到相同的G≥0.80阈值。现在我在查看跨benchmark的方差分解对比，item_id的百分比从MMLU的60.4%逐步下降到GSM8K的28.3%，而prompt×item则呈现相反的梯度从3.2%上升到11.8%，这正是推理型benchmark可复现性成本更高的核心发现。\n\n接着我在整理各benchmark达到G≥0.80所需的items数量——MMLU需要480个，HellaSwag需要550个，ARC-C需要650个，GSM8K则需要900个——以及跨模型的方差分解数据，其中model×item的贡献率在各benchmark间保持一致的20%以上水平。\n\n现在我要逐个benchmark运行D-study预测，计算达到目标信度所需的样本量，同时检验不同benchmark间的可分离性，特别是基础设施与方法交互项的差异。\n\n这是论文的核心创新发现。接下来设计多硬件可分离性G-study实验，涉及多个GPU类型、精度、提示词、种子和温度的组合，来验证硬件与方法论交互效应的显著性。\n\n实验结果显示硬件×温度交互在文本精确匹配下达到0.83%，这与不同GPU微架构中浮点运算的非关联性一致，而在二值评分下硬件×方法论交互小于0.2%，因为GPU硬件差异导致的数值偏差在二值评分时被大幅削弱。\n\n某些边界项目（模型输出概率接近0.5的题目）对数值精度敏感，这解释了硬件×项目交互达到1.2%的原因，但这是项目条件相关的现象，而非硬件×方法论的交互。\n\n在文本精确匹配评分下，硬件×温度达到0.83%是因为精确匹配对生成过程中任何微小概率差异都很敏感，而温度参数放大了这种差异——高温度下采样更随机，GPU数值差异的影响被扩大。硬件对整体方差结构的影响极小（0.12%），这验证了基础设施因素和方法因素在聚合层面是可分离的，与之前孤立因素研究的隐含假设一致。\n\n现在我在查看补充表格和热力图，这些展示了基础设施与方法论交互项的分布，几乎所有二元评分的单元格都低于0.5%，直观地证实了可分离性的成立。\n\n接下来我需要规划第四个实验，它将对前三个实验的合并数据进行17项综合分析，包括将模型作为分面的广义可靠性研究、自助法置信区间、D研究投影和项反应理论对比。\n\n预期结果显示跨多个数据集的大量记录分析，IRT和广义可靠性理论在项目数量上存在显著差异（比率在4.2到4.65之间），以及各种统计方法之间的最大差异百分比都在3%以下。\n\n现在我在查看二元数据防御指标，包括伯努利相关性、零模拟结果和自助法与刀切法置信区间的比较，同时评估MMLU项目配置变化的影响。功率分析表明交互作用检测的高功率（99.8%），最小可检测交互作用为1.2，而对数概率评分显示53.2%的项目级变异和0.572的G值。\n\n排名稳定性分析表明温度不会改变模型排序，肯德尔τ为0.94，这表明条件间的一致性很强。GPU成本分析估计MMLU需要48小时，GSM8K需要135小时，高阶交互作用仅占3.2%。实践者协议建议最少3个提示、4个种子和0.0温度。现在我在解释IRT与G理论之间的4.4倍差距机制——IRT将反应建模为项目难度和人员能力的函数，将所有非项目因素（提示、种子、顺序）归入误差项。\n\n而G理论则将提示×项目、种子×项目和顺序×项目等作为独立的系统性方差来源分离，要求G系数反映跨所有这些维度的一致性。这就是为什么需要510个项目而不是116个——额外的394个项目用于捕捉IRT模型忽略的那些方差维度，这4.4倍的差距本质上是多维度可靠性的代价。\n\n现在看bootstrap置信区间的结果，模型×项目的区间[21.3, 27.0]不包含0，这是因为4个模型×200个项目的设计规模提供了充分的统计功效来检测5%以上的效应。固定vs随机效应的敏感性仅有2.8%的变化，因为虽然温度只有3个设计点，但在G理论中这个选择只影响是否将温度方差纳入分母，而温度本身的方差很小，所以这个选择的影响有限。对于二元数据，伯努利分布与观测数据的相关性为0.68，说明约46%的项目方差可以归因于伯努利过程。\n\n在14K个项目的完整MMLU上，G系数达到0.943，这直接回应了关于MMLU不可靠的误读——我们的发现反映的是子样本的成本，而非MMLU本身的问题。对数概率的项目方差贡献略低于二元数据，因为它作为连续度量能捕捉二元截断所丢失的细微差异。\n\n现在我在整理跨基准的IRT与G理论项目需求对比，以及各方差成分的自助法置信区间，特别是模型×项目交互作用的显著性与基础设施×方法交互作用的非显著性。\n\n接下来是九项方法论防御的完整通过情况，以及针对不同目标信度水平（0.70/0.80/0.90）的D研究再现性预算，最后计算出MMLU约48小时、GSM8K约135小时的每模型GPU成本估算。\n\n我还在整理两个关键可视化：一张对比图展示IRT需要116项而G理论通过分解提示词和种子维度扩展到510项，以及一张温度参数热力图显示二元评分在不同温度下的信度衰减，文本精确匹配则在任何温度下都完全失效。\n\n现在进入17项分析的执行规划，按依赖关系分三波进行，第一波包括自助法置信区间、刀切法、固定随机效应敏感性检验等独立分析...\n\nWriting analysis plan... The larger 14B models show slightly higher item_id accuracy than 8B variants because they have more stable perception of item difficulty—their broader knowledge means correctness depends more on the item's inherent difficulty rather than gaps in model capability, though the difference is modest at +2.3pp given MMLU's wide difficulty distribution. The variance hierarchy remains unchanged because it reflects the evaluation design structure rather than model strength, so even reasoning distillation in DeepSeek-R1 doesn't alter the relative variance distribution across prompts, seeds, and items. MMLU主要考察知识而非推理——reasoning distillation的效果在GSM8K上会更明显(future work)。现在我在对比8B和14B模型的方差分解结果，计算每个方差成分的变化量和显著性，最终得出scale是方差结构的二阶调节因素而非一阶驱动因素的结论。接下来要在RoBERTa上进行fine-tuning的G-study实验，验证方差层级在fine-tuning和inference之间是否存在质变，这会涉及大量的参数组合运行。\n\n现在看到fine-tuning的方差分解结果，item_id的主导地位从inference时的60%下降到32%，而seed×lr和data_shuffle这些训练时的方差源在inference中是不存在的，说明fine-tuning引入了完全不同的方差结构。\n\nseed影响参数初始化和dropout mask，导致不同模型在特定item上的预测差异；learning rate控制优化的激进程度，可能导致item级别的系统性差异；data shuffle改变了训练样本的呈现顺序，进一步影响最终的模型参数。\n\nfine-tuned RoBERTa在SST-2和MNLI上的准确率超过90%，大多数item都被正确分类，这压缩了item间的难度差异，使得seed和learning rate引入的decision boundary变异成为可比较的方差来源，因此item_id的贡献降至31.7%。GPU和精度的交互作用仅占0.4%，与inference的结论一致——硬件因素在fine-tuning中同样可以忽略。\n\n现在我在设计一个对比表格，展示inference和fine-tuning的方差分解结果，突出item_id从60.4%下降到31.7%，以及seed×lr作为fine-tuning中的新主导因素达到28.4%。我还在准备一个并排的堆积柱状图来可视化这个regime change。\n\n为了验证这些发现，我对4个NLU任务各运行432次fine-tuning实验，采用全因子设计，然后在test set上评估每个模型并收集item级别的二元预测，最后运行G-study来分解方差。\n\n我会把seed、lr、data_shuffle、gpu和precision作为facets，与inference的方差结构对比来量化regime change的程度。这个分析仅限于encoder-only的分类任务，因为decoder fine-tuning需要更多计算资源。\n\n另外，我还在设计一个实验来测试LayerCast（FP16存储+FP32计算）作为MMLU上的额外precision条件，量化这种mitigation策略能降低多少方差。\n\n现在看到LayerCast确实将precision效应降低了65%，因为它的混合精度策略在保持接近FP32数值精度的同时，获得了接近FP16的速度和显存优势。在MMLU这类知识检索任务上，这种精度改进显著减少了item级别的翻转现象。\n\n不过LayerCast无法完全消除precision效应，因为FP16存储中的权重量化误差在某些极端边界情况下仍会导致结果翻转。由于LayerCast只改变数值精度而不影响其他因素，所以对prompt处理、seed采样和temperature scaling等其他方差源没有影响，这解释了为什么G系数从0.618提升到0.641。\n\n现在我需要通过补充表格来展示LayerCast的方差分解效果，对比有无LayerCast时的precision×item从2.8%降至1.0%以及precision主效应从1.4%降至0.5%的改进。我的分析方法是在原有的factorial design基础上加入LayerCast作为第四个precision level，然后比较这两种配置下的方差分解结果，以评估LayerCast是否是足够有效的precision缓解策略。\n\n接下来我要设计MATH基准测试的扩展实验，在200个题目上测试chain-of-thought推理在引入额外方差时的表现，这将是跨越五个基准的第五个扩展。预期结果显示单个模型和题目交互占22.1%，prompt与题目交互占13.5%，温度与题目交互占14.2%，种子与题目交互占9.8%，剩余方差占24.6%，同时计算出相应的G系数和D研究指标。\n\n现在我看到了扩展梯度的具体数值——MMLU达到60.4%，HellaSwag是50.4%，ARC Challenge是42.1%，GSM8K是28.3%，MATH是22.1%。这些数据显示MATH的item_id方差最低，原因在于MATH题目需要多步数学推导（通常5到10步），每一步都是潜在的随机分支点，当温度大于零时，chain-of-thought的随机性导致同一题目在不同运行中可能采取完全不同的推理路径，有些路径正确有些则错误。\n\n温度和item的交互效应（14.2%）对MATH的影响是MMLU的近7倍，这反映了MATH对采样随机性的极端敏感性。提示词格式的影响也很显著（13.5%），因为少样本示例的选择直接决定了模型采用代数法、几何法还是穷举法，不同策略在不同题目上的表现差异巨大。残差项达到24.6%，高于其他基准，这是因为chain-of-thought引入了大量无法归因的随机变异——即使所有条件相同，重复运行也会因采样随机性产生不同结果。广义可重复性系数仅为0.328，说明这个模型在MATH上的表现极不稳定。\n\nD-study分析表明需要1150个题目才能达到G≥0.80的可靠性标准，这是MMLU所需480个题目的2.4倍。这对实践有重要意义——当前研究通常只用MATH的一个子集（500题）进行评估，但我们的分析表明这可能不足以支撑可靠的模型比较。即使扩展到更多题目，排序的单调性保持完美（秩相关=1.0），但方差范围扩大到38.3个百分点，进一步证实了方差结构的关键作用。\n\n现在我在构建一个包含五个基准的完整梯度表，从知识检索（MMLU）到数学证明（MATH），展示从60.4到22.1的完美单调递减。我运行了与之前实验一致的因子设计，对所有五个基准进行了G-study对比，计算了秩相关性，并更新了D-study的样本量需求。\n\n对于MATH的评估，我需要实现链式思维解析来判断生成的逐步解答是否精确匹配或等价，这部分比较复杂。现在我在验证方差分量的内部一致性，检查各个实验中的方差成分是否大致相加到100%。\n\nexp-001的交叉模型实验各分量加总接近100%，但exp-002的HellaSwag单模型实验还缺少约9%的方差来源，可能是温度的主效应或其他我遗漏的因素。\n\n在G-study中，完整的方差分解应该包括所有主效应、二阶交互作用以及更高阶的交互，但实际报告时通常只突出显示超过1%的分量，其余的合并为\"其他\"。与其纠缠于每个分解